course: dictionary
lesson: 3806
title: Foreign: from another country, or just strange
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Foreign means from or belonging to another country, not your own. Context helps you see this. «Ruth was a young widow from Moab, a foreign land, who would not leave her mother-in-law Naomi.» The clues are «from Moab» and «land», so Moab is a place far from home, and foreign means from that other country.
explain: Foreign can also mean strange or unknown to you, even when nothing is far away. «The traveller greets the Rat with a gesture of courtesy that had something foreign about it.» The gesture was new or odd to the reader, so it felt unfamiliar. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Foreign money works the same way. «A small number on a foreign price tag does not mean it is cheap.» The price is in another country's money, so you must think what it is worth in your own money before you decide if it is cheap.
fact: Foreign means from or belonging to another country, not your own.
fact: Foreign can also mean strange or unknown to you.
fact: A foreign object is something in a place where it should not be, like dirt in an eye.
fact: Foreign currency is money used in another country, so you must change it to find its value.
exercise: «Ruth was a young widow from Moab, a foreign land.» What does foreign land mean here? | *A land in another country, not her own | A land with lots of water | A land where everyone is her family | A land with no people
exercise: In first aid, a speck of dirt stuck in an eye is called what? | *A foreign object | A foreign language | A foreign country | Foreign currency
exercise: «A small number on a foreign price tag does not mean it is cheap.» What should you think about before you decide? | *What it is worth in your own money | How many words are on the tag | Nothing, it is always cheap | Whether the tag is a nice colour
test: «The traveller greets the Rat with a gesture that had something foreign about it.» Here foreign most likely means | Loud and cheerful | *Strange or unfamiliar | Very hungry | From the Rat's own family
test: For a child living in England, which is a foreign country? | Your own house | *France | England | Your own street
exam: Which clue in «Ruth was a young widow from Moab, a foreign land» tells you foreign means from another country? | *from Moab | widow | young | would not leave
